\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Core notation used throughout the introduction and later methodological chapters.}}{5}{table.1.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Problem families covered by the dissertation and the corresponding evaluation focus.}}{7}{table.1.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Positioning of the present dissertation relative to adjacent literatures. The thesis sits closest to evaluator-guided program search, but its experimental logic also depends on benchmarked routing, hyper-heuristic reasoning, and conservative empirical methodology.}}{18}{table.2.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Completed empirical phases discussed in the final dissertation. A crossed model-tier study was scoped but is treated here as future work rather than as part of the completed evidence base.}}{21}{table.3.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Manual adjudication of the strongest novelty spikes in the phase-4 transfer-era recipes. The heavier replay-aware recipes show more genuine functional adaptations than the simple rotating baseline, but substantial churn remains even in the stronger recipes.}}{27}{table.4.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Phase-5 family summaries across the first replicated routing campaign. Lower deltas are better because all routing endpoints are lower-is-better gap metrics.}}{30}{table.5.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Key phase-6 TSP replay-mechanism comparisons. Positive deltas are worse because the metric is lower-is-better transfer gap.}}{31}{table.5.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Phase-7 operator-discovery summary. The search generates recurrent operator families, but no operator survives the full validation pipeline.}}{34}{table.6.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Phase-8 adaptive-portfolio summary. Lower deltas are better because the primary endpoint is held-out TSPLIB gap.}}{35}{table.6.2}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Phase-9 bounded CVRPLIB X split. The train/held-out separation is fixed before the campaign and is not reopened during solver evolution.}}{36}{table.6.3}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Phase-9 held-out results on bounded real-world CVRP. Lower penalized gap is better.}}{36}{table.6.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Recurring patterns across the completed phases. The positive signals are real, but each is paired with a concrete limiting factor rather than with an unrestricted win narrative.}}{38}{table.7.1}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Assessment of the working hypotheses. The evidence supports adaptation, but in a bounded and family-sensitive form.}}{39}{table.7.2}%
